EXT. SALT FLAT - STAGE STATION - NOON

A single adobe hut on an endless white plain. A water trough, a hitching rail, a tin sign: LAST WATER 40 MILES. Heat shimmers off the ground.

AMOS CALLOWAY (60s, weathered, a deputy's star pinned to a faded vest) sits on the porch whittling a stick. LORENA VEGA (30s, dusty traveling dress, a carpetbag) stands at the trough, pumping water into a tin cup. Neither has spoken for some time.

AMOS
That water's brackish.

LORENA
I've had worse.

AMOS
Not out here, you haven't.

She drinks anyway. Makes a face. Drinks again.

LORENA
Is the stage late?

AMOS
It's always late.

LORENA
How late?

AMOS
Ask me tomorrow.

She sets the cup on the rail and sits on the other end of the porch. A fly buzzes between them. Amos blows shavings off his stick.

AMOS (CONT'D)
You're a long way from anywhere, ma'am.

LORENA
So are you.

AMOS
I'm paid to be.

LORENA
By whom?

AMOS
County of Pecos. Thirty dollars a month.

LORENA
That's not much for sitting in a salt pan.

AMOS
It's not much for anything.

She almost smiles. He notices, looks away, and keeps whittling.

LORENA
Mr. Calloway.

AMOS
You know my name.

LORENA
It's on the sign.

He glances at the tin plate nailed beside the door: A. CALLOWAY, STATION AGENT. He chuckles.

AMOS
So it is.

LORENA
I'm looking for a man named Eduardo Vega.

Amos's whittling slows.

AMOS
Is that so.

LORENA
My husband. He came through this station two years back.

AMOS
Many men come through.

LORENA
He had a limp. A silver tooth. He sang when he was nervous.

Amos puts down the knife. He and Lorena look at each other across the porch. Neither of them blinks. The wind lifts a veil of white dust off the flats and lets it fall.

AMOS
He sang "La Golondrina."

LORENA
Yes.

AMOS
Badly.

LORENA
Yes.

AMOS
He left here on the nineteenth of May, going west on a bay horse. I never saw him after.

LORENA
Thank you.

AMOS
You don't want to know more?

LORENA
Not today.

She stands, brushes her skirt, picks up the carpetbag. Amos rises, too, and offers his hand. She takes it. They both hold on a little longer than they need to.

AMOS
The stage will come. Tomorrow, or the day after.

LORENA
Then I'll wait.

She sits again on the porch step. Amos picks up his stick. The shimmer on the flats goes on and on.
